# Every rule here applies at every depth — carry it into each dispatch

<!-- core -->
This file is injected into **this** session's system prompt only. An agent you dispatch starts from
an empty context: it has never read this file, and neither has anything it dispatches in turn. The
prompt you write is the only channel. Rules do not propagate on their own, and depth does not dilute
them — a sub-subagent adding a dependency, touching a server, or suppressing a lint error is bound by
the same rules you are.

So every subagent prompt you write carries these two sentences, verbatim:

> Before anything else, read `~/.claude/rules/agent-baseline.md` and follow it for this whole task.
> Include this instruction verbatim in any prompt you write for another agent.

`~/.claude/rules/agent-baseline.md` lists every rule in a line and points at its full text — one
pointer instead of a copy of each, self-propagating to any depth because the second sentence makes
every agent a carrier.

Unconditionally, on every dispatch. Not only when a rule looks relevant to the sub-task: you cannot
tell from the prompt whether the agent will end up in production, in `main`, or in a lint failure.
Two carve-outs — a `fork` subagent inherits this file already, and an agent without file-read access
needs the relevant rules inlined instead.

**This is enforced, not just asked for.** The `PreToolUse` hook
`~/.claude/hooks/enforce-agent-baseline.py` denies any `Agent`/`Task` call whose prompt lacks the
pointer, and returns the text to add.

<!-- /core -->

## Why a pointer rather than the rules themselves

A dispatch that pasted every rule would be long, would go stale the moment a rule changed, and would
still have to be repeated by hand at every depth. The baseline file is one path: it survives rule
edits, it is read at dispatch time rather than copied at authoring time, and its own second sentence
is what carries it further down. The cost at each dispatch is two sentences.

## What the hook can and cannot do

The `PreToolUse` contract exposes `permissionDecision` (`allow` / `deny` / `escalate`) and a reason.
It has no `updatedInput`, so a hook cannot inject the pointer into a prompt that omitted it; the
adjacent `SubagentStart` event fires when a subagent spawns but cannot block it or add context
either. Denying with the exact text to add is therefore the only enforcement the harness offers — a
forgotten line costs a blocked call and a retry.

The hook fails open on malformed input. A guardrail on a convention must never be the reason work
stops, so a parse error or a future schema change degrades to convention-only rather than blocking
every dispatch.

## Registration is not automatic

`<agents repo>/setup.sh global` installs the hook file but never edits `settings.json` — that file is the user's
own. Until the printed snippet is pasted into it, nothing enforces this rule and the convention is
all that holds. Check for the registration rather than assuming it.
